<InternalLayout title="Best-Worst Scaling for intensity coding" description="Methodology reference for the BuyerVoice intensity-coding approach. Why we replaced 1/2/3 ordinal rating with Best-Worst Scaling, what BWS measures, how the pipeline runs, and the citations behind the choice.">
  <article class="bws-doc">
    <header class="doc-header">
      <p class="overline">Methodology / Magnitude coding</p>
      <h1>Best-Worst Scaling for intensity coding</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Why we replaced the 1/2/3 ordinal intensity scale with Best-Worst Scaling in May 2026, what BWS actually measures, how the pipeline runs it, and the citations behind the choice. Read this when a colleague asks "what does the BWS score mean?" or "why did we change?"
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>The headline</h2>
      <p>
        Every BuyerVoice study produces three layers of coding per meaning unit: <strong>thematic</strong> (what topic), <strong>direction</strong> (+ / - / ~), and <strong>intensity</strong> (how strongly the code is held). Direction is unchanged. The intensity dimension used to be a 1/2/3 ordinal rating assigned per (participant, code) pair. As of May 2026, intensity is produced by <strong>Best-Worst Scaling</strong> at the verbatim level, partitioned by question grouping. The output is a continuous score in [&minus;1, +1] per verbatim, aggregated to per-attribute mean, top-tier rate, and distribution shape.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <h4>One-sentence version</h4>
        <p>
          Humans (and AI coders mirroring human conventions) are unreliable on absolute ordinal judgments at 3+ tiers but are dramatically more reliable when making relative judgments. BWS replaces "score this on 1 to 3" with "of these four, which is most intense and which is least?" and aggregates the picks into continuous per-verbatim scores.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>Why we retired the 1/2/3 ordinal scale</h2>
      <p>
        The prior version of this pipeline asked dual AI coders to rate each (participant, code) pair on a 1/2/3 ordinal scale anchored to behavioral exemplars: 1 = "brief mention," 2 = "elaborated," 3 = "narrative incident." On paper it was defensible: it followed Saldana's (2021) magnitude-coding terminology, the anchors were concrete, and the dual-coder + arbiter pattern matched the rest of the pipeline.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice it had three compounding problems.
      </p>

      <div class="problem-list">
        <div class="problem">
          <span class="problem-num">1</span>
          <div class="problem-body">
            <h3>Cohen's unweighted kappa is the wrong statistic for ordinal data</h3>
            <p>
              We were reporting Cohen's kappa on the 1/2/3 disagreements. Cohen's unweighted kappa is designed for nominal categories where all disagreements are equally serious. On an ordinal 1/2/3 scale, a 2-vs-3 disagreement is far less serious than a 1-vs-3 disagreement, and unweighted kappa treats them identically. The right statistics are <strong>weighted kappa</strong> (Cohen, 1968) or <strong>Krippendorff's ordinal alpha</strong> (Krippendorff, 2018). Switching to weighted kappa or ordinal alpha on the existing data typically lifts the reported number by 0.10-0.25 with no change to the coding itself. The original 0.30-0.40 was partly an artifact.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="problem">
          <span class="problem-num">2</span>
          <div class="problem-body">
            <h3>3+ tier ordinal human coding is empirically hard</h3>
            <p>
              Even with the right statistic, the underlying problem persists. The norm across content analysis, sentiment annotation, and computational linguistics is that human raters achieve high agreement on binary distinctions, decent agreement on 3-tier nominal, and degrade rapidly past 3 ordinal tiers without explicit anchors. Mohammad &amp; Bravo-Marquez (2017) and the broader WASSA shared-task work moved <em>away</em> from direct rating scales for exactly this reason: human raters are unreliable on absolute ordinal judgments. Our coders averaging kappa ~0.35 was the published norm, not a pathology.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="problem">
          <span class="problem-num">3</span>
          <div class="problem-body">
            <h3>The distributions were heavily skewed</h3>
            <p>
              Even after a binary collapse (high = 2 or 3, low = 1) the distribution typically ended up 90%+ in one bucket. UK Neobanks shipped with the binary collapse and most theme bars rendered ~98% "high intensity." The stacked-bar visualization that depended on intensity variation added visual noise without analytical information. The data was carrying less signal than the methodology promised.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Two reasonable responses to this situation: (a) keep 1/2/3, switch to weighted kappa, accept the modest improvement; or (b) change the measurement instrument itself. The literature on text-annotation intensity is unusually clear on which option pays back better.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>Why Best-Worst Scaling is the right alternative</h2>
      <p>
        Best-Worst Scaling was developed by Jordan Louviere and Gary Woodworth (1991) as a choice-modeling method. It was adapted to text annotation by Saif Mohammad and collaborators in the SemEval and WASSA shared tasks (Mohammad &amp; Bravo-Marquez, 2017). The premise: <strong>relative judgments are reliable, absolute ordinal judgments are not.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Instead of asking the coder "is this verbatim a 1, 2, or 3?", BWS presents the coder with four verbatims at a time and asks two questions:
      </p>
      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li>Which of these four is the <strong>most intense</strong> expression?</li>
        <li>Which is the <strong>least intense</strong>?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Across many tuples, every verbatim ends up with a continuous score in [&minus;1, +1] computed as <code>(n_best &minus; n_worst) / n_appearances</code>. A verbatim picked as "most intense" every time it appeared and never as "least intense" scores +1. The opposite scores &minus;1. Even-distribution scores 0.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-amber">
        <h4>The reliability difference is dramatic, not marginal</h4>
        <p>
          Mohammad &amp; Bravo-Marquez (2017) report that BWS achieves substantially higher inter-annotator consistency than direct rating scales on the same intensity construct. Our pipeline expectation is Krippendorff's ordinal alpha of 0.65-0.80 per question grouping, vs the 0.30-0.40 Cohen's kappa the prior pipeline was hitting. The improvement comes from changing what the coder is asked to do, not from training the coder harder.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>What "question grouping" means and why partitioning matters</h2>
      <p>
        BWS is run separately for each <strong>question grouping</strong>, not pooled across the whole study. A question grouping is one report section: drivers of selection, rejection reasons, current pain points, switching forces, retained value, etc.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reason: <strong>intensity is question-relative.</strong> The verbatims that come from a "what frustrated you" question are answering a different emotional prompt than the verbatims from "what attracted you to this product." Frustration language is naturally more intense than praise language across most populations. Pooling them in one BWS pass would give us scores that mostly reflect "frustration vs praise," not "intensely-held frustration vs casually-held frustration." That would be a bias artifact, not a finding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Question groupings are declared in <code>study_config.json</code> under a <code>bws_groupings</code> block. Each grouping lists the question IDs whose verbatims feed one BWS pass. Each grouping produces its own scale; we don't compare BWS scores across groupings.
      </p>
      <div class="example-card">
        <h4>Example: a B2C banking study's question groupings</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Drivers of selection</strong> &mdash; Q4 ("what attracted you to your primary bank?")</li>
          <li><strong>Rejection reasons</strong> &mdash; Q6 ("which other banks did you consider but not choose?") + Q7 ("why didn't you pick them?")</li>
          <li><strong>Current pain points</strong> &mdash; Q10 ("what frustrates you about your bank today?")</li>
          <li><strong>Switching push forces</strong> &mdash; Q12 ("what would push you to leave?")</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Each of these is one self-contained BWS pass. A theme like "fees" can appear in both "rejection reasons" and "current pain points"; it gets two separate BWS scores, one per context. That's the right answer, not a bug.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>Why max-of-peaks aggregation, not mean-of-all-verbatims</h2>
      <p>
        Before walking through how the pipeline runs, one design choice that took us several iterations to land on: each participant contributes exactly one BWS score per attribute, and that score reflects their <strong>peak</strong> expression, not the mean of every time they raised the theme.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two reasons this is the right convention, and not the verbatim-level aggregation our first implementation used.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Participant over-weighting.</strong> If we aggregate at the verbatim level, a participant who mentioned an attribute three times contributes three data points; a participant who mentioned it once contributes one. Talkative participants get multiple votes. The standard convention in survey methodology and qualitative content analysis (Saldana, 2021) is one data point per participant per attribute. Aligning BWS aggregation with that convention removes the talkativeness artifact.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Peak intensity is what's psychologically operative, not average intensity.</strong> Three converging literatures support taking the participant's peak verbatim as the relevant signal, not their mean:
      </p>
      <ul class="lit-list">
        <li><strong>Peak-end rule (Kahneman, Fredrickson, Schreiber &amp; Redelmeier, 1993).</strong> People remember experiences not by their average but by their peak intensity and how the experience ended. A participant who had one severe overdraft incident encodes their relationship with fees around that peak, not around the average affect across every fee interaction. Their peak verbatim is closer to what would surface at the point of purchase than the mean of their verbatims.</li>
        <li><strong>Gricean conversational maxims (Grice, 1975).</strong> The Maxim of Quantity says: make your contribution as informative as required, and no more. A speaker who has produced a fully intense expression of a topic violates this maxim if they keep restating it at the same intensity. The cooperative move is to background later mentions.</li>
        <li><strong>Given-new contract (Haviland &amp; Clark, 1974; Clark &amp; Haviland, 1977).</strong> Once information is in the conversational common ground, the speaker treats it as "given" and produces it in compressed form. Repeated mentions are by-products of conversational structure, not additional intensity data.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All three converge on the same prediction: in a semi-structured interview, a participant's most intense expression is the analytically meaningful one. Mean-of-verbatims dilutes this signal; max-of-peaks captures it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>How the two-stage pipeline runs</h2>
      <p>
        <code>run_bws_intensity.py</code> auto-chains after <code>run_coding.py</code> completes. For each question grouping it runs two stages: max-identification, then BWS on the surviving peaks.
      </p>

      <h3>Stage 1: Max-identification</h3>
      <p>
        For each (participant, attribute) pair within a question grouping where the participant has 2+ coded meaning units assigned to the attribute, two AI agents independently pick which mention is the participant's most intense expression of that attribute. Single-mention pairs skip this step entirely; their one mention is the peak by definition.
      </p>
      <p>
        Within-participant max-identification is a much more constrained task than full-pool BWS. The comparison is between 2-6 verbatims from the same speaker about the same attribute, so writing style, vocabulary, and baseline emotional register are held constant. Picks are typically high-reliability because the intensity signals (specific incidents, named amounts, narrative structure) are easy to compare when the speaker and topic are fixed.
      </p>
      <div class="step-list">
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">1</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Filter to multi-mention pairs</h4>
            <p>For each grouping, identify (participant, attribute) pairs with 2+ verbatims. Single-mention pairs skip Stage 1; their one verbatim is the peak.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">2</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Two Sonnet coders pick the peak independently</h4>
            <p>M1 (Sonnet, temp 0, narrative lean) and M2 (Sonnet, temp 0.3, specificity lean) each pick the most intense mention from the set. Persona divergence creates productive independence: M1 weights narrative elaboration and emotional framing; M2 weights concrete specifics (named amounts, temporal markers, named outcomes).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">3</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Opus arbiter resolves disagreements</h4>
            <p>For multi-mention pairs where M1 and M2 picked different verbatims, M3 (Opus, temp 0, balanced) sees the same set and both coders' reasoning and decides. Typically 10-20% of multi-mention pairs.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">4</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Output one peak per (participant, attribute) pair</h4>
            <p>After Stage 1, every mentioning participant has exactly one peak verbatim per attribute in this grouping. The Stage 2 pool is the union of all peaks across the grouping.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Stage 2: BWS on peaks with participant-disjoint tuples</h3>
      <p>
        Each peak is now compared against other participants' peaks via Best-Worst Scaling. Two design rules matter here.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Participant-disjoint tuples.</strong> Each tuple of 4 peaks must come from 4 distinct participants. If two peaks from the same participant (different attributes) appeared in the same tuple, the BWS comparison would partly reflect that speaker's shared style, undoing the within-participant work Stage 1 just did. The participant-disjoint constraint ensures every tuple is a fair comparison across four different people. Feasibility: the constraint holds as long as no single participant contributed more than 25% of the grouping's peaks, which is the typical case.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Attributes mixed within tuples.</strong> A tuple typically contains peaks for four different attributes (from four different participants). This makes the resulting BWS scores cross-attribute comparable within the grouping, which is what powers the 2x2 frequency &times; intensity scatter chart.
      </p>
      <div class="step-list">
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">5</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Generate participant-disjoint balanced tuples</h4>
            <p>Tuple size K=4. Replication r=4 (each peak appears in 4 tuples). Total tuples per grouping = N peaks. Constraint: no two peaks in the same tuple share a participant. The combinatorial design is generated deterministically.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">6</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Two Sonnet coders judge every tuple</h4>
            <p>B1 (Sonnet, temp 0, narrative lean) and B2 (Sonnet, temp 0.3, specificity lean) independently pick the most-intense and least-intense verbatim in each tuple. Two picks per tuple. No absolute scoring. Same coder personas as Stage 1 (M1's voice = B1's voice; same for M2 and B2).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">7</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Opus arbiter resolves disagreed tuples</h4>
            <p>Any tuple where B1 and B2 disagree on either pick (most, least, or both) goes to B3 (Opus, temp 0, balanced) for full re-resolution. Volume typically 10-20% of tuples.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">8</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Compute per-verbatim BWS scores</h4>
            <p>For each peak verbatim: <code>score = (n_picked_as_most &minus; n_picked_as_least) / n_appearances</code>. Range &minus;1 to +1. The score is calibrated against other participants' peaks, not against the participant's own non-peak mentions or other participants' filler.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">9</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Aggregate to attribute level (participant-level inputs)</h4>
            <p>Each participant contributes exactly one BWS score per attribute (their peak's BWS). Attribute-level mean is the mean of those per-participant peak scores. Top-tier rate is % of mentioners whose peak BWS exceeds +0.30. Distribution shape is the participant-level histogram across low / mid / high bands.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-row">
          <div class="step-circle">10</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Compute and report reliability</h4>
            <p>Stage 1: raw agreement rate and set-size-adjusted Cohen's kappa for max-id picks. Stage 2: Krippendorff's ordinal alpha and Spearman rank correlation between the two coders' per-verbatim score arrays, plus tuple-level agreement rate as a diagnostic. Reliability is measured pre-arbiter; the arbiter's role is to clean up final picks, not to inflate the reported reliability number.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

