    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>What the analyst sees in the report</h2>
      <p>
        Every report section anchored on a BWS grouping carries two visual elements.
      </p>
      <div class="visual-grid">
        <div class="visual-card">
          <h4>2x2 frequency &times; intensity scatter</h4>
          <p>X-axis: % of cohort mentioning the attribute. Y-axis: mean BWS score within the grouping. Quadrant lines at medians. Upper-right quadrant (high frequency &times; high intensity) gets the strategic callout box.</p>
          <p class="visual-note">This is the chart that powers the "what should we act on" conversation. Without intensity, the upper-right quadrant is just "most frequent." With BWS intensity, it's "frequent <em>and</em> deeply held."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="visual-card">
          <h4>Distribution-shape table</h4>
          <p>One row per attribute. Columns: % low (&lt; &minus;0.20) / % mid (&minus;0.20 to +0.20) / % high (&gt; +0.20). Same data the scatter plots, but it shows whether an attribute's intensity comes from a few standouts or uniformly weighted mentions.</p>
          <p class="visual-note">An attribute with 70 mentions evenly distributed across intensity bands tells a different story than an attribute with 18 mentions concentrated in the high band. Two flat bar charts would look the same.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>Trade-offs to be honest about</h2>
      <p>
        BWS is materially better than direct ordinal rating for our use case, but it has costs and caveats worth knowing.
      </p>

      <div class="tradeoff-list">
        <div class="tradeoff">
          <h4>Scores are relative within each grouping, not absolute</h4>
          <p>
            A study with 10 weakly-held rejection reasons still produces some verbatims at "high intensity" within that set. It just means highest <em>among</em> that set, not high in some universal sense. Methodology section needs to acknowledge this. Cross-study comparisons of raw BWS scores are not meaningful.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tradeoff">
          <h4>Each grouping needs enough verbatims to produce stable scores</h4>
          <p>
            With N verbatims and r=4 replication, individual-verbatim score variance scales with 1/&radic;r. Attribute-level means are stable; individual-verbatim noise is moderate. As a rule of thumb, groupings below ~30 verbatims produce noisy attribute scores. We typically have 100-300 verbatims per grouping, well above the floor.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tradeoff">
          <h4>Participant-level analysis is built in by design, not an aggregation choice</h4>
          <p>
            The two-stage pipeline produces one BWS score per (participant, attribute, grouping). This is the canonical participant-level intensity for that combination. Downstream analyses (quartile cross-tabs against behavioral outcomes, segmentation basis variables, "high-intensity mentioner" classification) read this score directly. No need to aggregate after the fact.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tradeoff">
          <h4>No published BWS-of-brand-verbatims study validates the predictive claim</h4>
          <p>
            The brand-equity literature establishes association <em>strength</em> as a behaviorally predictive dimension (Keller, 1993; Maison, Greenwald &amp; Bruin, 2004). The text-annotation literature establishes BWS as a more reliable instrument than direct rating (Mohammad &amp; Bravo-Marquez, 2017). The peak-end and Gricean literatures support max-of-peaks aggregation (Kahneman et al., 1993; Grice, 1975). The intersection &mdash; two-stage BWS-coded brand-interview verbatims predicting purchase, market share, or NPS &mdash; has not been peer-reviewed in head-to-head form. Our claim rests on construct validity from each adjacent literature, not on a direct empirical validation that doesn't exist.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tradeoff">
          <h4>Cost is lower than the original convention</h4>
          <p>
            The two-stage approach is materially cheaper than the single-stage version we originally documented. Stage 1 max-identification: ~$2-3 per study (only multi-mention pairs trigger Sonnet calls). Stage 2 BWS on peaks: ~$6-8 per study (fewer peaks than the full-verbatim pool means fewer tuples). Total typically $8-11 per study, vs $15-30 for the retired single-stage convention.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>How to interpret BWS scores in absolute terms</h2>
      <p>
        The relative-scoring property in the trade-off list above is the question every methodologically careful customer asks: "If +0.5 is just 'more intense than the others in this set,' how do I know the +0.5 in your rejection-reasons grouping is actually meaningful, and how do I compare it to a +0.5 in your pain-points grouping?"
      </p>
      <p>
        The honest answer is that BWS scores are calibrated <strong>within the question they came from</strong>. They are not absolute on some universal intensity scale. This is a property of BWS as a method, not a defect of our implementation: direct rating scales offer absolute anchors at the cost of being unreliable; BWS offers reliability at the cost of giving up absolute calibration. We build interpretive scaffolding around the relative scores so customers can take them as meaningful behavioral signals.
      </p>

      <h3>Five tactics, ranked by impact</h3>

      <div class="problem-list">
        <div class="problem">
          <span class="problem-num">1</span>
          <div class="problem-body">
            <h3>Anchor verbatims (the cleanest calibration, biggest investment)</h3>
            <p>
              Maintain a stable library of 4-6 anchor verbatims that span the intensity spectrum. Insert them into every BWS pass alongside the study's real verbatims. If the "very high" anchor scores +0.7 in study A and +0.4 in study B, you know study B's verbatims are absolutely more intense than study A's (they pushed the anchor down the scale), and you have a meaningful threshold to compare attribute-level means against. This is the practice the Mohammad / WASSA group uses for cross-study calibration. Setup cost: one round of analyst calibration. Per-study cost: trivial (~5 added verbatims per grouping).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="problem">
          <span class="problem-num">2</span>
          <div class="problem-body">
            <h3>Top-tier rate above a fixed threshold (cheap, works today)</h3>
            <p>
              Lead with the top-tier rate at a fixed absolute threshold (default +0.30), not the raw mean. "40% of fees mentions are in the top intensity tier" reads as absolute even though it is built from relative judgments. It says: across the relative judgments, 40% of the time a "fees" verbatim came up against three competitors, it got picked as the most intense one (or close to it). That is a real signal regardless of what the comparison set looked like. The threshold doesn't move across studies, which makes top-tier rate more robust for cross-study comparison than raw means.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="problem">
          <span class="problem-num">3</span>
          <div class="problem-body">
            <h3>Verbatim drilldown as the qualitative truth check</h3>
            <p>
              The BWS score is a sort order; the verbatim is the proof. When a customer asks "is +0.5 really intense?", the right response is: click the bar, read the top three verbatims that drove it. If they read as deeply held convictions with specific incidents and dollar amounts, the score is meaningful. If they read as casual asides, the score is telling you the comparison set was weak. The drilldown is the methodological honesty layer. Don't try to make BWS do work that the verbatims can do better.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="problem">
          <span class="problem-num">4</span>
          <div class="problem-body">
            <h3>Behavioral correlation as the predictive validity check</h3>
            <p>
              For any study where behavioral outcomes are measured (switching, NPS, purchase intent, retention), correlate per-participant BWS scores with the outcome. This converts "is +0.5 really intense?" into "do high-BWS mentioners actually behave differently?" Bin participants by BWS quartile for an attribute (e.g., fees), then show the switching rate per quartile. If Q4 mentioners switch at 41% while Q1 mentioners switch at 8%, the BWS scale has earned its meaning behaviorally, not by comparison to an external anchor. Always include this table in the methodology section when behavioral outcomes are in the data.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="problem">
          <span class="problem-num">5</span>
          <div class="problem-body">
            <h3>Distribution shape over single statistics</h3>
            <p>
              The shape of the BWS distribution within an attribute carries information the mean obscures. Right-skewed (long tail of high scores) means a small but intense advocate/detractor base. Even distribution means widely shared at moderate intensity, often category-level rather than differentiating. Left-skewed means surface complaint, not a deep driver. The distribution-shape table below the 2x2 scatter does this work; the report narrative needs to teach the reader to read the shape, not just the height.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout callout-amber">
        <h4>What to say when a customer asks the threshold question</h4>
        <p>
          "BWS scores are calibrated within the question they came from. A +0.5 means this verbatim was consistently picked as more intense than others answering the same kind of question, not that it crosses some absolute threshold. To make absolute claims, we lean on three things: (a) the distribution shape within the question, (b) the actual verbatims behind the score, which you can read by clicking through, and (c) the behavioral correlation in the same data, where we can show that high-BWS mentioners are X times more likely to have switched or churned. The score is a sort order; the proof is in the quotes and the behavior."
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Default practice and when to escalate</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>For every study (default):</strong> lead with top-tier rate (tactic 2) as the headline statistic, lean on the verbatim drilldown (tactic 3) for the qualitative truth check, and always render the distribution-shape table (tactic 5) below the 2x2 scatter. These three work today with the existing pipeline at no additional cost.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>When behavioral outcomes are measured:</strong> always run the BWS-by-outcome correlation table (tactic 4) and surface it in the methodology section. This is the strongest answer to "do high BWS scores really mean anything" because it shows them meaning something in this customer's data.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>When cross-study comparison matters or a new study type opens:</strong> invest in anchor verbatims (tactic 1). Pilot a 4-anchor library on one grouping, validate that the anchor scores are stable across pilot vs full runs, and decide whether to make it standard. The setup cost is one analyst day; the payback is calibrated cross-study comparison going forward. This is the long-term answer to "what does +0.5 mean across our portfolio of studies."
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>When a colleague asks "what does the BWS score mean?"</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <h4>The plain-English answer</h4>
        <p>
          "The BWS score is a continuous intensity score from &minus;1 to +1 for each verbatim within its question grouping. It's built from many pairwise comparisons against other verbatims in the same grouping. A score above +0.30 means the verbatim was repeatedly judged among the most intense expressions of that kind of content. A score below &minus;0.30 means it was repeatedly judged among the least intense. The attribute-level number you see on the chart is the average of all that attribute's verbatim scores within the grouping."
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        If they push harder on what "intense" means: it's about specific incidents, named amounts, strong evaluative language, personal narrative, length, and elaboration &mdash; the signals defined in the BWS prompt. The coder is not assigning anything; they are picking the most and least intense among four candidates in front of them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
      <h2>Legacy studies and back-compatibility</h2>
      <p>
        Five studies were coded with the retired 1/2/3 ordinal layer and continue to ship their original deliverables.
      </p>
      <ul class="legacy-list">
        <li><strong>UK Neobanks</strong> &mdash; two-wave switcher + non-switcher study. Carries <code>final_codes_with_intensity.json</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Retail Banking CIBC</strong> &mdash; 300-participant B2C switcher study.</li>
        <li><strong>Intercom Fin</strong> &mdash; two-cohort B2B study.</li>
        <li><strong>Retinol Switching</strong> &mdash; 240-participant B2C upgrader/defector study.</li>
        <li><strong>HR Syndicated 2026</strong> &mdash; the syndicated B2B build (if it had reached the intensity pass before the switchover).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These remain as-is. Their reports show the older stacked-by-intensity bar pattern. If any of them comes back for a follow-up analysis, the BWS pass can be run retroactively on the frozen thematic + direction coding; the new output ships as <code>final_codes_with_bws_v2.json</code> alongside the original.
      </p>
      <p>
        Historical studies that pre-date the magnitude layer entirely (HR Leaders BGR, HR Leaders BambooHR, CEP &amp; Brand, JTBD Switch) carry <code>final_codes.json</code> without direction or intensity. They are left untouched.
      </p>
    </section>

