    <section class="doc-section doc-section-refs">
      <h2>Citations</h2>
      <div class="refs">
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Louviere, J.J. &amp; Woodworth, G.G.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">1991</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Best-Worst Scaling: A Model for the Largest Difference Judgements</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">University of Alberta Working Paper</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Original formulation of Best-Worst Scaling as a choice-modeling method. The methodological foundation for our use of BWS to measure intensity through relative judgments.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Mohammad, S.M. &amp; Bravo-Marquez, F.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">2017</div>
          <div class="ref-title">WASSA-2017 Shared Task on Emotion Intensity</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">Proceedings of the EMNLP Workshop on Computational Approaches to Subjectivity, Sentiment and Social Media Analysis</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Established BWS as the gold standard for fine-grained intensity annotation in computational linguistics. Demonstrated that BWS reliably outperforms direct ordinal rating scales on inter-annotator consistency. The empirical foundation for our May 2026 retirement of 1/2/3 ordinal rating.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Saldana, J.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">2021</div>
          <div class="ref-title">The Coding Manual for Qualitative Researchers (4th ed.)</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">SAGE Publications</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Canonical source for the term "magnitude coding" and the conceptual frame of supplemental codes for direction and intensity. Saldana treats magnitude codes as analytic markers; we operationalize the intensity dimension via BWS for reliability reasons. Also the source for the "one data point per participant" convention in qualitative content analysis prevalence reporting.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Kahneman, D., Fredrickson, B.L., Schreiber, C.A. &amp; Redelmeier, D.A.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">1993</div>
          <div class="ref-title">When more pain is preferred to less: Adding a better end</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">Psychological Science, 4(6), 401-405</div>
          <div class="ref-role">The peak-end rule: people remember experiences by peak intensity and how they ended, not by averages. Foundational empirical support for max-of-peaks aggregation at the participant level. A participant's relationship with a brand attribute is encoded around their peak experience, which is what BWS-on-peaks captures.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Grice, H.P.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">1975</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Logic and conversation</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">In P. Cole &amp; J.L. Morgan (Eds.), Syntax and semantics 3: Speech acts (pp. 41-58). Academic Press.</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Conversational maxims, particularly the Maxim of Quantity. Speakers do not repeat fully intense expressions at the same intensity because it violates the cooperative principle. Supports the max-of-peaks framing: a participant's first or most intense mention is the analytically relevant signal; later mentions are conversationally backgrounded.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Haviland, S.E. &amp; Clark, H.H.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">1974</div>
          <div class="ref-title">What's new? Acquiring new information as a process in comprehension</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">Journal of Verbal Learning and Verbal Behavior, 13(5), 512-521</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Original formulation of the given-new contract. Once information is in the conversational common ground, the speaker treats it as "given" and produces it in compressed form. Repeated mentions of the same topic are typically less affective and less elaborated than the initial mention. Foundational psycholinguistic finding underlying the max-of-peaks convention.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Clark, H.H. &amp; Haviland, S.E.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">1977</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Comprehension and the given-new contract</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">In R. Freedle (Ed.), Discourse production and comprehension (pp. 1-40). Ablex.</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Extended treatment of the given-new contract. Establishes the cognitive and discourse-level mechanisms underlying compression of repeated mentions.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Miles, M.B., Huberman, A.M. &amp; Saldana, J.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">2020</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Qualitative Data Analysis: A Methods Sourcebook (4th ed.)</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">SAGE Publications</div>
          <div class="ref-role">The frequency &times; intensity argument: the same frequency at different intensities is a different strategic signal. The analytical basis for adding intensity as a magnitude dimension at all.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Krippendorff, K.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">2018</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Content Analysis: An Introduction to Its Methodology (4th ed.)</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">SAGE Publications</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Defines Krippendorff's alpha for nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio data. Publishes the 0.80 (reliable) and 0.667 (tentative conclusions) thresholds we use for BWS ordinal alpha. Argues against unweighted Cohen's kappa for ordinal data.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Cohen, J.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">1968</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Weighted kappa: Nominal scale agreement with provision for scaled disagreement or partial credit</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">Psychological Bulletin, 70(4), 213-220</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Weighted kappa formulation for ordered categories. The correct statistic if you wanted to stick with rating-based intensity; complementary to Krippendorff's alpha.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Keller, K.L.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">1993</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Conceptualizing, Measuring, and Managing Customer-Based Brand Equity</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">Journal of Marketing, 57(1), 1-22</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Names favorability, strength, and uniqueness as the three predictive dimensions of brand associations. Direction operationalizes favorability; BWS intensity operationalizes strength. Theoretical home for our magnitude-coding design.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Krishnan, H.S.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">1996</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Characteristics of memory associations: A consumer-based brand equity perspective</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">International Journal of Research in Marketing, 13(4), 389-405</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Operationalizes brand-association strength via memory-network measures. Empirical support for treating association strength as a discriminating dimension between high-equity and low-equity brands.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Maison, D., Greenwald, A.G. &amp; Bruin, R.H.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">2004</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Predictive Validity of the Implicit Association Test in Studies of Brands, Consumer Attitudes, and Behavior</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">Journal of Consumer Psychology, 14(4), 405-415</div>
          <div class="ref-role">Three experiments showing that the strength of brand-attribute associations predicts brand preference, product usage, and behavior beyond explicit presence/absence. The strongest indirect empirical support for treating intensity as a behaviorally meaningful dimension.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="ref">
          <div class="ref-authors">Tirunillai, S. &amp; Tellis, G.J.</div>
          <div class="ref-year">2012</div>
          <div class="ref-title">Does Chatter Really Matter? Dynamics of User-Generated Content and Stock Performance</div>
          <div class="ref-journal">Marketing Science, 31(2), 198-215</div>
          <div class="ref-role">In UGC-to-sales prediction, volume dominates, polarity matters asymmetrically, and intensity is a smaller marginal step beyond polarity. Honest context for how much incremental predictive validity intensity carries on top of frequency + direction.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
